{"id":"cluster-administration-networking","corpus":"k8s","title":"Cluster Networking","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/networking/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Cluster Networking"},{"kind":"p","text":"Networking is a central part of Kubernetes, but it can be challenging to understand exactly how it is expected to work. There are 4 distinct networking problems to address:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Highly-coupled container-to-container communications: this is solved by Pods and localhost communications."},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod-to-Pod communications: this is the primary focus of this document."},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod-to-Service communications: this is covered by Services."},{"kind":"li","text":"External-to-Service communications: this is also covered by Services."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes is all about sharing machines among applications. Typically, sharing machines requires ensuring that two applications do not try to use the same ports. Coordinating ports across multiple developers is very difficult to do at scale and exposes users to cluster-level issues outside of their control."},{"kind":"p","text":"Dynamic port allocation brings a lot of complications to the system - every application has to take ports as flags, the API servers have to know how to insert dynamic port numbers into configuration blocks, services have to know how to find each other, etc. Rather than deal with this, Kubernetes takes a different approach."},{"kind":"p","text":"To learn about the Kubernetes networking model, see here."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Kubernetes IP address ranges"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes clusters require to allocate non-overlapping IP addresses for Pods, Services and Nodes, from a range of available addresses configured in the following components:"},{"kind":"li","text":"The network plugin is configured to assign IP addresses to Pods."},{"kind":"li","text":"The kube-apiserver is configured to assign IP addresses to Services."},{"kind":"li","text":"The kubelet or the cloud-controller-manager is configured to assign IP addresses to Nodes."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Cluster networking types"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes clusters, attending to the IP families configured, can be categorized into:"},{"kind":"li","text":"IPv4 only: The network plugin, kube-apiserver and kubelet/cloud-controller-manager are configured to assign only IPv4 addresses."},{"kind":"li","text":"IPv6 only: The network plugin, kube-apiserver and kubelet/cloud-controller-manager are configured to assign only IPv6 addresses."},{"kind":"li","text":"IPv4/IPv6 or IPv6/IPv4 dual-stack:"},{"kind":"li","text":"The network plugin is configured to assign IPv4 and IPv6 addresses."},{"kind":"li","text":"The kube-apiserver is configured to assign IPv4 and IPv6 addresses."},{"kind":"li","text":"The kubelet or cloud-controller-manager is configured to assign IPv4 and IPv6 address."},{"kind":"li","text":"All components must agree on the configured primary IP family."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes clusters only consider the IP families present on the Pods, Services and Nodes objects, independently of the existing IPs of the represented objects. For example, a server or a pod can have multiple IP addresses assigned to its interfaces, but only the IP addresses in node.status.addresses or pod.status.ips are considered when implementing the Kubernetes network model and defining the cluster type."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How to implement the Kubernetes network model"},{"kind":"p","text":"The network model is implemented by the container runtime on each node. The most common container runtimes use Container Network Interface (CNI) plugins to manage their network and security capabilities. Many different CNI plugins exist from many different vendors. Some of these provide only basic features of adding and removing network interfaces, while others provide more sophisticated solutions, such as integration with other container orchestration systems, running multiple CNI plugins, advanced IPAM features etc."},{"kind":"p","text":"See this page for a non-exhaustive list of networking addons supported by Kubernetes."},{"kind":"h2","text":"What's next"},{"kind":"p","text":"The early design of the networking model and its rationale are described in more detail in the networking design document. For future plans and some on-going efforts that aim to improve Kubernetes networking, please refer to the SIG-Network KEPs."}],"lemmas":{"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"clustered":["clustered","cluster"],"networking":["networking","network"],"networkings":["networkings","networking"],"networks":["network","networks"],"centrals":["central","centrals"],"parts":["part","parts"],"parted":["parted","part"],"parting":["parting","part"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"challenging":["challenging","challenge"],"challengings":["challengings","challenging"],"challenges":["challenge","challenges"],"challenged":["challenged","challenge"],"understands":["understands","understand"],"understanding":["understanding","understand"],"understanded":["understanded","understand"],"understood":["understood","understand"],"exactlies":["exactlies","exactly"],"expected":["expected","expect"],"expecteds":["expecteds","expected"],"expects":["expects","expect"],"expecting":["expecting","expect"],"works":["work","works"],"worked":["worked","work"],"working":["working","work"],"distincts":["distincts","distinct"],"problems":["problem","problems"],"addressing":["addressing","address"],"addressed":["addressed","address"],"highlies":["highlies","highly"],"coupled":["coupled","couple"],"coupleds":["coupleds","coupled"],"couples":["couple","couples"],"coupling":["coupling","couple"],"containers":["container","containers"],"communications":["communication","communications"],"solved":["solved","solve"],